From d6ee9ecb6f58adb0b7587ce79eb82c63caea2699 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: brunduk <nikolai.viktorovich@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 4 Aug 2026 16:00:20 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] 360 viewer: directory playlists, transport keys, and
 full-rate 8K60

Playlist
--------
HELLO_XR_VIDEO360 may now name a directory: every video in it plays once, sorted by name,
wrapping at the end. Each track tears down and rebuilds the whole video chain (Video360,
staging ring, plane images, conversion pass, skybox texture) since the next file may have a
different resolution and projection - the mode banner reprints per track. A file that will
not open is skipped, not fatal. A single file keeps looping forever as before.

The rebuild exposed a latent assumption: OpenVideoTexture began with m_cmdBuffer.Begin(),
which asserts unless the buffer is freshly Initialized - true at startup, false when
arriving mid-playback from a rendered frame (state Executable). Clear() first.

Transport keys (playercontrol.{h,cpp})
--------------------------------------
space pause/resume, [ ] speed steps 0.125x-4x, 1 normal, n next track, q/ESC quit. The
stdin thread in main.cpp (previously "any key quits") now feeds these; on a pipe there are
no keys and EOF still ends the run, so the timed test invocations behave as always.

Speed works by accumulating wall time into the playback clock scaled by the current rate,
so pausing is rate 0 and there is no discontinuity to patch up. The clock also now starts
at the first frame's pts rather than 0, which stops files whose timestamps do not start at
zero from having their opening skipped. Decoding never pauses - back-pressure through the
buffer ring holds the decoder naturally.

Frame ring: 5 -> 8 buffers
--------------------------
At 8K60 the decoder averaged full rate but its per-frame jitter (keyframes) kept draining
a 3-frame cushion: the renderer found nothing new on ~25% of frames ("renderer starves"
in the stats), which the user saw as stutter. Three more buffers (+126 MB host RAM at 8K,
negligible at 4K) absorb it completely.

Measured end to end, 8K VR180 stereo 60fps file, after this commit:
  decode 59-61 fps | upload 60.0 fps | 0 starves | render thread 6.3 ms
i.e. the full file rate. The remaining flicker the user reports is the G2's 60Hz
low-persistence backlight strobe itself - only 90Hz fixes that (lab, cap. 04).
